  {
    "id": "variable/recursive-expansion",
    "note": "A context value containing %b% expands recursively (up to MAX_VARIABLE_DEPTH).",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "Hello %a%",
    "context": {
      "a": "%b%",
      "b": "World"
    },
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "Hello World"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "variable/undefined-stays-literal",
    "note": "An unresolved %var% is left verbatim at render time (a warning at validate time, not an error).",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "Hi %missing%",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "Hi %missing%"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "set/global-scope-inside-group",
    "note": "A #set on its OWN line INSIDE an enumeration is extracted GLOBALLY (like extract_set_directives), so %x% in the other option sees it. rng:last picks the %x% option ⇒ 'A'. Regression for the directive-global-pass gap.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "{\n#set %x% = A\n|%x%}",
    "rng": "last",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "A"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "set/macro-re-rolls-at-every-reference",
    "note": "A #set is a macro: the value is substituted at each %x% and its enumeration re-rolls. Two draws are consumed — seq[0]=0 then seq[1]=1 — which is the observable difference from #def below. A first-option RNG could NOT tell the two apart.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set %x% = {a|b}\n%x%-%x%",
    "rng": {
      "sequence": [
        0,
        1
      ]
    },
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\na-b"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def/rolled-once-and-held",
    "note": "Same template and the SAME rng sequence as set/macro-re-rolls-at-every-reference. A #def is rendered once and frozen, so one draw is consumed and seq[1] is never reached.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#def %x% = {a|b}\n%x%-%x%",
    "rng": {
      "sequence": [
        0,
        1
      ]
    },
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\na-a"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def/rolled-once-covers-permutations",
    "note": "Roll-once is not bracket-specific: a permutation freezes too, so both references shuffle identically. Its #set twin below shares the template and the rng sequence and shuffles twice — the pair is the pin, neither half means much alone.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#def %x% = [<sep=\", \"> a|b]\n%x% / %x%",
    "rng": {
      "sequence": [
        1,
        0,
        1,
        0
      ]
    },
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\na, b / a, b"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def/resolves-against-runtime-context",
    "note": "The roll runs AFTER the context is merged, so a definition can read a runtime variable. Rolling it earlier (where collapse-once sat) would freeze the literal text %who%.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#def %x% = %who%-{a|b}\n%x% %x%",
    "context": {
      "who": "Acme"
    },
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\nAcme-a Acme-a"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def/runtime-outranks-a-definition",
    "note": "Runtime beats every local, #def included; the definition is then never rolled at all.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#def %x% = {a|b}\n%x%",
    "context": {
      "x": "RUNTIME"
    },
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\nRUNTIME"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def/dependency-through-a-set-alias",
    "note": "%b% never mentions %a%: it reaches it through the macro %s%, expanded at reference time and therefore invisible in %b%'s own text. Ordering on direct references alone froze %b% with %a% unexpanded and the plural block vanished for want of a numeric count. Declaration order is deliberately reversed.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#def %b% = %s% {plural %s%: item|items}\n#set %s% = %a%\n#def %a% = {1|4}\n%b%",
    "locale": "en",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\n\n1 item"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "set/macro-re-shuffles-a-permutation",
    "note": "Same template and rng sequence as def/rolled-once-covers-permutations; a macro re-shuffles at the second reference.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set %x% = [<sep=\", \"> a|b]\n%x% / %x%",
    "rng": {
      "sequence": [
        1,
        0,
        1,
        0
      ]
    },
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\na, b / b, a"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-html-full-elements-is-literal",
    "note": "The permutation-config HTML guard, found missing in a port (spintax-js#58): a leading <li> is HTML, not config, when the tag shape matches AND its closing </li> appears later in the body — the elements keep their markup. Without the guard the whole first tag becomes the single-separator config and the output is mangled markup. rng 'first' reverses a two-element shuffle, hence two before one.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<li>one</li>|<li>two</li>]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "<li>two</li> <li>one</li>"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-tag-without-closing-is-sep",
    "note": "The guard's other half: no </li> anywhere after, so <li> is NOT HTML — it is a config, and a config with none of the four known keys is the single-separator form (the whole string becomes sep AND lastsep). A purely-alphabetic separator is space-padded on join, which is why the output reads 'two li one', not 'twolione'.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<li>one|two]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "two li one"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-self-closing-is-html",
    "note": "A self-closing tag needs no later closer to be HTML — the trailing slash alone passes the guard.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<br/>x|y]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "y <br/>x"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-html-with-attributes-renders",
    "note": "The renderer's guard accepts attributes: <li class=\"x\">…</li> is HTML and renders literally. The VALIDATOR disagrees on this same template — see validate/perm-config-html-with-attributes-is-flagged; the two fixtures together pin the family-wide split exactly as all three engines ship it.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<li class=\"x\">one</li>|<li>two</li>]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "<li>two</li> <li class=\"x\">one</li>"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-key-substring-is-still-a-sep",
    "note": "The key-form test is `\\b(?:minsize|maxsize|sep|lastsep)\\s*=` — a word boundary AND an equals sign, not a substring scan. 'separator' contains 'sep' but is no key, so the whole string is the single-separator form and pads like any alphabetic sep. A substring detector flips this into key-form-with-all-defaults and the word silently vanishes from the output (spintax-js#58, the Pascal port's Pos() test).",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<separator>a|b]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "b separator a"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-embedded-key-with-value-is-still-a-sep",
    "note": "The word-boundary half of the same rule: 'xminsize=2' carries minsize= as a SUBSTRING but not at a boundary, so it is no key-form — the whole string is the separator (not purely alphabetic, so it glues bare). A substring detector does worse than default here: it finds 'minsize' inside the word, parses '=2', and renders a genuine minsize-2 permutation. The validator flags this template — validate/perm-config-embedded-key-is-flagged pins that half.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<xminsize=2>a|b]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "bxminsize=2a"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "set/malformed-head-cr-directive-survivor",
    "note": "Render twin of validate/directive-check-cr-survivor-satisfies: the parser's four-terminator anchors let '#set %x% = A' sit between a CR and a LF, so it is consumed as a real directive; the malformed head keeps its CR and stays text.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set broken\r#set %x% = A\n%x%",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "#set broken\r\nA"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "set/last-definition-wins",
    "note": "Two definitions of one name: the LAST wins (Map overwrite), so %x% renders B. First-wins resolution renders the self-referential first value instead. The verdict side (definition.duplicate-name) is pinned in validate.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set %x% = %x%\n#set %x% = B\n%x%",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\n\nB"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "set/duplicate-cycle-renders-lenient",
    "note": "Last-wins turns %a% into a mutual cycle with %b%, and render stays lenient (#57): the 51-hop budget starts at %a%, its parity leaves %b% as the unresolved literal. First-wins resolution renders 'plain' and never meets the cycle.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set %a% = plain\n#set %a% = %b%\n#set %b% = %a%\n%a%",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\n\n%b%"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-key-without-equals-ignored",
    "note": "Render twin of validate/permutation-key-without-equals-is-not-a-key: 'maxsize 2' without '=' is not a key, maxsize stays null, and all three elements render. An extractor with an optional '=' renders a 2-element subset here.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<sep=\"-\" maxsize 2>a|b|c]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "b-c-a"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-ascii-ws-around-equals",
    "note": "The \\s* around a config '=' is the full JS class: a LINE FEED between 'maxsize' and '=1' still parses, so exactly one element renders. Skipping only space and tab reads maxsize as null and renders all three.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<sep=\"-\" maxsize\n=1>a|b|c]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "b"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-unterminated-quote-kills-config",
    "note": "The closing '>' scan is QUOTE-AWARE (findConfigEnd): an unterminated quote swallows every later '>', no config end is found, and the '<…>' text falls through into the first element of a default-separator permutation. An engine that reads the config anyway invents a separator or a maxsize the reference never saw.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<sep=\"- maxsize=1>a|b|c]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "b c <sep=\"- maxsize=1>a"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "perm/config-sep-key-matches-inside-word",
    "note": "SEP_RE has NO word boundary — only a (?<!last) lookbehind — so once key form is chosen (the standalone sep=\"-\" satisfies the \\b gate), the FIRST sep= match wins even inside 'xsep', and the separator is '.'. Asymmetric with the \\b in the key-form gate, and pinned as-is: an engine that requires a boundary in the extractor too picks '-'.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "[<xsep=\".\" sep=\"-\">a|b]",
    "rng": "first",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "b.a"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "plural/count-conditional-resolves",
    "note": "A conditional reaching the COUNT slot resolves before the numeric test, so the block renders. Every engine used to expand variables into the slot and stop there: the conditional survived, failed the numeric test, and the block was ERASED while validate() reported nothing -- and plural.count-macro exempts conditionals precisely BECAUSE they resolve before plurals.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set %flag% =\n#set %n% = {?flag?1|2}\n{plural %n%: one|two}",
    "locale": "en",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\n\ntwo"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "plural/count-conditional-truthy-branch",
    "note": "The other branch of the same shape: both are resolved, not just the one that happens to be second.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set %flag% = yes\n#set %n% = {?flag?1|2}\n{plural %n%: one|two}",
    "locale": "en",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\n\none"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "plural/count-conditional-nested",
    "note": "A branch may hold another conditional; the count slot resolves them all the way down.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set %a% =\n#set %b% = 1\n#set %n% = {?a?9|{?b?2|3}}\n{plural %n%: one|two}",
    "locale": "en",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\n\ntwo"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "plural/count-conditional-inverted",
    "note": "Truthiness in the count slot is the ordinary rule, inversion included -- an undefined name is falsy, so {?!missing? takes the then-branch.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set %n% = {?!missing?2|1}\n{plural %n%: one|two}",
    "locale": "en",
    "postProcess": false,
    "expect": {
      "output": "\ntwo"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "plural/count-enum-still-erases",
    "note": "NEGATIVE control for the case above. Only conditionals resolve early; enumerations run AFTER plurals, so an enum in the count slot still reaches the numeric test intact and erases the block. Resolving the branch by rendering it instead of substituting it would spin this to 1 and invent a count no engine has.",
    "kind": "deterministic",
    "op": "render",
    "template": "#set %n% = {1|2}\n{plural %n%: one|two}",
